SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/ethiopia/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House said Article 93 allows suspension of political and democratic rights during a state of emergency, and that authorities used emergency powers in late 2021 and early 2022 to crack down on media houses and arrest online and broadcast journalists.","locator":"country report text around Article 93 and the state-of-emergency discussion","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Established rights monitor with direct country-report analysis and specific legal-context claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 period and the emergency powers still affecting the year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to federal authorities under Abiy's government; the report separates legal basis from the implementation of restrictions, but not every action is individually ordered by him in the text.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that the six-month state of emergency was lifted in February 2022, so some restrictions were inherited from the prior emergency period."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Two years of internet shutdowns: people in Tigray, Ethiopia, deserve better","publisher":"Access Now","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.accessnow.org/press-release/two-years-internet-shutdowns-tigray/","claim":"Access Now said authorities had kept Tigray under an internet shutdown for two years, preventing communication, access to information, and accountability over violence.","locator":"opening paragraphs of the press release","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous rights-monitor reporting on a specific shutdown and its political effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and explicitly about the shutdown's duration up to that date.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Ethiopian authorities; this supports the de facto command path even though the underlying written order is not exposed in the public source.","contrary_evidence":["Access Now is an advocacy source, so it is strong on the existence and effect of the shutdown but weaker on the precise internal command chain."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed NEBE materials, Freedom House, Access Now, Reuters/CPJ/HRW items already in the index, and election-law context pages.
- I did not find a public 2022 national-election or transfer event, so the national contestability / succession / term-limit gap is not recoverable from 2022 materials.
- I did not find a public primary document naming the full internal command chain for the Tigray blackout or media actions, so that part remains only partly resolved.
- I rejected broader election-law pages and general governance material where they only restated the legal framework without adding 2022 conduct evidence.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct written orders or court records showing who authorized the Tigray shutdown and related media restrictions.
- A national-election or transfer event in 2022 does not exist, so that gap stays not recoverable rather than merely under-researched.
- More search would help only for command-path documents, not for a non-existent 2022 national election test.
